WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.390
The nominal training
window is twenty eleven.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.990
January through June, twenty twelve,
supplies validation for comparing choices.

3
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.310
July through December remains
the reserved final test.

4
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.500
A random mixture of early and later
observations would change the historical

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.050
forecasting question.

6
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.230
These windows stay fixed
across later comparisons.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.750
Final test outcomes do not guide
the current feature choices,

8
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.210
model settings or
exploratory error analysis.

9
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.550
We still need to check when each preceding
stage was ready to support a forecast.

10
00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:42.664
The final training hour runs from
eleven at night until midnight.

11
00:00:43.074 --> 00:00:48.284
Under our convention, its outcome becomes
available at midnight, as January begins.

12
00:00:49.034 --> 00:00:52.804
Now consider a validation target
starting at that same midnight.

13
00:00:53.214 --> 00:00:56.654
A one hour lead requires its
forecast at twenty three hundred,

14
00:00:56.654 --> 00:00:59.584
when the last training
interval has only begun.

15
00:01:00.154 --> 00:01:04.034
A model built using that interval's
completed outcome could not have existed

16
00:01:04.034 --> 00:01:05.304
for this issue time.

17
00:01:05.754 --> 00:01:09.464
Sorting target starts alone
misses this availability problem.

18
00:01:11.442 --> 00:01:15.292
We keep the midnight
observation in the raw source,

19
00:01:15.362 --> 00:01:17.552
but exclude it from the model partitions.

20
00:01:18.482 --> 00:01:22.452
The first validation target now
starts at one in the morning.

21
00:01:22.942 --> 00:01:26.112
Its one hour ahead forecast
is issued at midnight.

22
00:01:26.822 --> 00:01:30.862
That is when the final training outcome
becomes available under the current

23
00:01:30.862 --> 00:01:32.272
offline convention.

24
00:01:32.902 --> 00:01:37.922
The rule is that preceding outcomes
must be ready no later than the first

25
00:01:37.922 --> 00:01:39.752
evaluation issue time.

26
00:01:40.202 --> 00:01:45.592
The target starts and forecast issue times
play different roles in this inequality.

27
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.960
This exercise treats the source
hour label as an interval start.

28
00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.520
It treats its outcome as available at
interval end, with zero reporting delay.

29
00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.140
It also assumes zero model building
delay at the stage boundary.

30
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.620
A practical system with either
delay needs a later readiness time.

31
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.740
The source does not specify timezone
or daylight saving disambiguation,

32
00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.840
so we retain naive date and hour labels.

33
00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.900
The calculation is internally explicit.

34
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.960
It is not documentary proof
of physical elapsed hours,

35
00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.160
real reporting latency
or production readiness.

36
00:02:27.495 --> 00:02:31.385
Validation is used to compare
and select modelling choices.

37
00:02:32.035 --> 00:02:36.205
Its final hourly interval finishes at
midnight, at the beginning of July.

38
00:02:36.915 --> 00:02:41.075
A test target starting at that midnight
would require an issue time an hour

39
00:02:41.075 --> 00:02:41.745
earlier.

40
00:02:42.275 --> 00:02:44.095
We exclude that existing midnight row too.

41
00:02:44.095 --> 00:02:49.245
The first test target starts at one,
with its issue at midnight.

42
00:02:49.775 --> 00:02:54.635
The two excluded source identifiers are
eight thousand six hundred forty six and

43
00:02:54.635 --> 00:02:56.045
thirteen thousand four.

44
00:02:56.315 --> 00:03:00.795
We retain their identities and exclusion
reasons without using reserved test

45
00:03:00.795 --> 00:03:02.725
outcomes to select models.

46
00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:09.247
The split function first assigns targets
before January first to training.

47
00:03:09.757 --> 00:03:10.867
For later targets,

48
00:03:11.237 --> 00:03:14.107
it checks issue time by
subtracting the forecast lead.

49
00:03:14.657 --> 00:03:17.647
An issue before validation
readiness is excluded.

50
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:22.567
It repeats the same logic at July
first before assigning the final test.

51
00:03:23.237 --> 00:03:26.907
The supported windows include their
starts and exclude their ends.

52
00:03:27.537 --> 00:03:30.687
Dates outside twenty eleven
and twenty twelve are rejected.

53
00:03:31.317 --> 00:03:32.297
After partitioning,

54
00:03:32.297 --> 00:03:36.417
a separate guard compares the last
preceding interval end with the first

55
00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:39.967
evaluation issue time,
checking the same contract again.

56
00:03:41.937 --> 00:03:45.737
The frozen training partition contains
eight thousand six hundred forty five

57
00:03:45.737 --> 00:03:46.687
rows.

58
00:03:47.117 --> 00:03:49.357
Validation contains four thousand
three hundred fifty seven,

59
00:03:49.357 --> 00:03:54.427
and reserved test contains four
thousand three hundred seventy five.

60
00:03:55.537 --> 00:03:59.487
Two existing observations are
recorded as boundary exclusions.

61
00:03:59.837 --> 00:04:03.894
Together those counts recover all
seventeen thousand three hundred seventy

62
00:04:03.894 --> 00:04:05.767
nine acquired observations.

63
00:04:06.577 --> 00:04:10.407
The three model partitions contain
seventeen thousand three hundred seventy

64
00:04:10.407 --> 00:04:11.667
seven rows.

65
00:04:12.337 --> 00:04:16.797
None of the one hundred sixty five
unrecorded slots becomes a new row or an

66
00:04:16.797 --> 00:04:17.947
exclusion identifier.

67
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:25.880
Keep the saved split unchanged and try
a hypothetical availability calculation.

68
00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.000
The final training
interval ends at midnight.

69
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:33.100
Reporting takes twenty minutes and
building takes another fifteen.

70
00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.740
Readiness is now twelve thirty five.

71
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.700
A one o’clock target needs an issue
at midnight, which is too early.

72
00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:45.240
A two o’clock target needs an issue
at one, after the model is ready.

73
00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:50.220
The first feasible hourly request
moves because availability changed,

74
00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.640
not because random splitting or a
convenient gap size became preferable.

75
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.970
The comparison is small
enough to write directly.

76
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.770
Subtract the lead from the target
start to get the issue time.

77
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.810
Add reporting delay to
the training interval end,

78
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.570
then add the build delay.

79
00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.930
The issue must be at or
after that readiness time.

80
00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.270
Under the saved zero delay convention,

81
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.990
midnight readiness permits
the one o’clock target.

82
00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.870
Under the hypothetical thirty
five minute delay, it does not.

83
00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.090
This is a sensitivity example.

84
00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.070
No frozen membership file is
regenerated by explaining it.

85
00:05:30.192 --> 00:05:34.932
Suppose the actual service later confirms
that reporting always takes twenty

86
00:05:34.932 --> 00:05:35.562
minutes.

87
00:05:36.232 --> 00:05:40.962
Can we keep the old availability claim
simply because the split is chronological?

88
00:05:41.872 --> 00:05:44.442
Pause and use the issue time comparison.

89
00:05:45.132 --> 00:05:48.982
A real contract change requires
an explicit preparation revision.

90
00:05:49.612 --> 00:05:52.562
It may exclude additional
recorded boundary observations,

91
00:05:53.132 --> 00:05:55.862
which is different from
filling missing source hours.

92
00:05:56.372 --> 00:06:01.422
Keep the existing saved assumptions and
membership intact for current comparisons.

93
00:06:01.932 --> 00:06:06.062
A revised experiment needs its own
declared contract and evidence.
